sections.body:
Literary French differs from everyday French in vocabulary, syntax and rhythm:

- tenses: passé simple, passé antérieur, and in very formal style the **imparfait du subjonctif** (*qu'il fût*, *qu'il eût*) — to recognise only;
- word order: inverted subjects (*Ainsi parla le roi.*), adjectives placed before the noun for effect (*une sombre histoire*);
- vocabulary: *demeurer* (to remain, to live), *songer* (to think, dream), *s'en aller*, *l'aube* (dawn), *le crépuscule* (dusk), *naguère* (not long ago), *jadis* (in times past);
- figures of speech (*les figures de style*): *la métaphore* (*un océan de blé*), *la comparaison* (*blanc comme neige*), *la personnification* (*la ville s'éveille*), *l'hyperbole* (exaggeration), *la litote* (understatement — *Va, je ne te hais point* = I love you), *l'antithèse*, *l'anaphore* (repetition at the start of lines).
